<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Murphy is a 40-year-old man with post-traumatic stress disorder, much improved after completing Cognitive Processing Therapy last month, and chronic insomnia managed without medication, who asked for a same-day visit. Three days ago he developed a sore throat, followed by a runny and blocked nose and a dry cough. His highest home temperature was 37.8 °C. A home COVID-19 antigen test yesterday was negative. Several coworkers have had colds. For the past two nights the cough and blocked nose have woken him, and he is worried about losing the sleep he has regained. He had thought of buying a &quot;nighttime&quot; cold and flu syrup and a decongestant tablet, but wanted to check first because his care plan asks him to avoid sedating sleep aids. So far he has used only honey and throat lozenges. He has no breathlessness, chest pain, ear pain, or facial pain. His nightmares have not increased, his mood is steady, and he has had no thoughts of self-harm. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Three days ago, starting with a sore throat</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Throat, nose, and chest</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Three days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Clear runny nose, blocked nose, scratchy throat, dry cough</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Mild to moderate; sleep broken on two nights</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Cough and blockage worse when lying down at night</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Coworkers with colds; home COVID-19 antigen test negative yesterday</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Honey and lozenges help a little; no other remedies taken</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Mild tiredness. Denies fever above 37.8 °C, breathlessness, chest pain, ear or facial pain, and sputum</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

